1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,468
Every morning, someone copies new form entries into a

2
00:00:03,468 --> 00:00:06,246
spreadsheet, then pastes a summary into Slack.

3
00:00:06,306 --> 00:00:09,199
Miss one, and a customer never hears back.

4
00:00:09,259 --> 00:00:11,914
What if that busywork ran by itself?

5
00:00:12,164 --> 00:00:14,971
Fix found: Activepieces.

6
00:00:15,031 --> 00:00:19,298
Building blocks for your busywork: when this happens, do that.

7
00:00:19,358 --> 00:00:23,695
Its README calls it an open-source Zapier replacement, with over

8
00:00:23,695 --> 00:00:26,120
twenty-five thousand GitHub stars.

9
00:00:26,370 --> 00:00:30,372
Each flow starts with a trigger: a schedule, a webhook, or an

10
00:00:30,372 --> 00:00:31,604
event in an app.

11
00:00:31,664 --> 00:00:35,887
Then come the steps, each one a piece: Google Sheets, an AI

12
00:00:35,887 --> 00:00:37,831
prompt, or a little code.

13
00:00:37,891 --> 00:00:41,555
Test each step, publish, and it runs by itself.

14
00:00:41,805 --> 00:00:46,275
One. Connect your apps: hundreds of ready-made pieces, with

15
00:00:46,275 --> 00:00:50,531
loops, branches, retries, and an HTTP step for the rest.

16
00:00:50,591 --> 00:00:54,978
Two. Keep a human in the loop. A flow can wait, or pause until

17
00:00:54,978 --> 00:00:57,371
someone approves, then carry on.

18
00:00:57,431 --> 00:01:02,027
Three. Let your AI assistant build it. The built-in MCP server

19
00:01:02,027 --> 00:01:06,205
lets Claude or Cursor create flows from a plain request.

20
00:01:06,455 --> 00:01:08,043
Now, the catch.

21
00:01:08,103 --> 00:01:12,561
The core is MIT licensed, but the enterprise code is under a

22
00:01:12,561 --> 00:01:17,088
commercial licence. Single sign-on, roles, audit logs and git

23
00:01:17,088 --> 00:01:18,899
sync need a paid plan.

24
00:01:18,959 --> 00:01:22,725
The one-command Docker install is for personal use on one

25
00:01:22,725 --> 00:01:26,863
machine. Real workloads need a database, a queue, file storage,

26
00:01:26,863 --> 00:01:29,271
and a worker for each running flow.

27
00:01:29,331 --> 00:01:32,714
And it's still before version one, so upgrades often bring

28
00:01:32,714 --> 00:01:33,860
breaking changes.

29
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:37,578
It's for teams who want automations on their own servers, and

30
00:01:37,578 --> 00:01:40,299
developers happy to write pieces in TypeScript.

31
00:01:40,359 --> 00:01:43,292
If you'd rather not look after a server, it's more than you

32
00:01:43,292 --> 00:01:43,711
need.

33
00:01:43,771 --> 00:01:47,604
Want an alternative? Take a look at n8n.

34
00:01:47,854 --> 00:01:51,858
To try it, run the one Docker command from their docs, open it

35
00:01:51,858 --> 00:01:54,892
in your browser, and create the first account.

36
00:01:54,952 --> 00:01:58,581
What problem should we fix next? Tell us in the comments.

